## Automating the verification

**Status: built and working.** Lives in `2-FSR-v2/automation/` (see
`automation/autonomous.md` for the full list of what runs without manual steps).
Every check
above is now a SQL assertion that runs from a laptop against the dev warehouse.
No notebook watching, no copy-pasting output.

### Why not Databricks Agents

The Agents UI (Knowledge Assistant, Genie, Information Extraction) is for
building end-user chatbots over data. Genie could translate "are any docs
stuck?" into SQL, but it is non-deterministic — the same question can produce
different SQL on different days, and a verification gate that sometimes
disagrees with itself is worse than no gate. Verification wants fixed queries
with fixed expected answers. So: plain SQL, checked into git, diffable.

Agents stay useful for the *product* side (letting users ask questions of FSR
data). They are the wrong tool for CI.

### How it works

Three things are reachable directly from the dev profile, which is what makes
this unattended:

| Capability | API | Verified |
|---|---|---|
| Query dev tables | SQL Statement Execution on warehouse `ai-pw-ser-ds-dev-sqlw` (`c383216f6af5c7c0`) | yes |
| Trigger pipeline jobs | `jobs/run-now` — `CAN_MANAGE` on all FSR v2 jobs | yes |
| Read driver logs | `jobs/runs/get-output` | yes |

So the loop is: trigger job → poll to terminal state → query tables → print
PASS/FAIL → non-zero exit on failure. Compute is serverless throughout, so
nothing waits on a cluster starting.

### Files

```
2-FSR-v2/automation/
  autonomous.md      what can run without manual steps
  verify_fsr_v2.py   CLI: `check` (assert only) and `run` (jobs + assert)
  checks.py          the assertions, one function each
  dbx_client.py      stdlib REST client, reads ~/.databrickscfg
  tracks.json        per-track tables, doc lists, and job params
```

`tracks.json` holds the same parameters as the widget blocks above, so the
notebook params and the automation cannot drift apart. The LiteLLM key is a
`${LITELLM_API_KEY}` placeholder resolved from the environment at launch — no
secret in the file.

### Usage

```bash
cd 2-FSR-v2/automation

# read-only, safe any time, touches nothing
python3 verify_fsr_v2.py check --track 1

# full unattended run: ddl -> p1 -> p2 -> vs -> check -> rerun p1,p2 -> compare
export LITELLM_API_KEY=sk-...
python3 verify_fsr_v2.py run --track 1
```

`run` also covers the idempotency test on its own: it snapshots
`(document_id, parsed_volume_path, chunk_count)` after the first pass, reruns
P1 and P2, and fails if anything moved. That was step 9 of Track 2 and step 6
of Track 1 — both now automatic.

### Checks implemented

Mapped one-to-one to the pass criteria, all against the real dev schema:

| Check | Replaces |
| --- | --- |
| expected docs completed | "all N docs have `metadata_status='completed'`" |
| no rows stuck `in_progress` | "no docs remain in_progress after P2 drains" |
| no failed docs (surfaces `metadata_error`) | manual failure scan |
| `primary_esn` populated | "primary_esn non-null for all docs" |
| `parsed_volume_path` populated | "parsed_volume_path populated" |
| all date columns match `YYYY-MM-DD` | manual date format eyeball |
| no completed doc older than `min_doc_year` | date-filter verification |
| equipment map row per completed doc | "equipment map rows present" |
| chunks exist per chunked doc | "chunk rows present for all docs" |
| no orphan chunks | new — catches chunks left behind by a reset |
| `embedding_dimension == 3072` | "embedding_dimension logged as 3072" |
| no empty `chunk_text` | retrieval sanity |
| idempotency snapshot compare | "chunk row count unchanged on rerun" |
| driver log greps: `P1 workers = 4`, `LLM batch i/N`, `[DATE-FILTER/` | steps 3 and 4 log inspection |

Sample output against the freshly reset dev tables — note it correctly flags
the empty table and passes everything else:

```
  [FAIL] expected docs completed (8)        8 not completed: [...=MISSING]
  [PASS] no rows stuck in_progress          metadata=0, chunk=0
  [PASS] no failed docs                     none
  [PASS] all date columns are YYYY-MM-DD    ok
  [PASS] no completed docs older than 2016  ok
  [SKIP] embedding dimension                no chunk rows yet, skipped
  12 checks, 1 failed
```

### Remaining gap

The concurrency / batching / date-filter checks parse driver stdout, and that is
the weak link: **the jobs run on serverless, which does not dependably return a
`logs` field from `runs/get-output`.** So these three checks may silently report
"pattern not found" even on a healthy run. Treat them as advisory until fixed.

The real fix is a few lines in P1 writing these as rows into `fsr_run_log_v2`
(`event_type`, `detail`), after which they become SQL like everything else and
we get run history for free. This is now worth doing rather than optional.

Retrieval smoke test (step 7 of Track 2) is not automated yet, but it is
unblocked: one-off serverless notebook runs work (verified), so a small notebook
that queries the VS index and returns results via `dbutils.notebook.exit` can be
triggered and read back the same way as everything else. No cluster needed.

### What is needed from you

Just the LLM key, once per verification cycle:

```bash
export LITELLM_API_KEY=sk-...
python3 verify_fsr_v2.py run --track 1
```

Everything else I can drive and read back myself — `check` is read-only and safe
to run against the tables at any time. See `2-FSR-v2/automation/autonomous.md`
for the full capability list.
